<!-- HEADER -->
<div class="header">
  <div class="eyebrow">Analytics Report</div>
  <h1>Lichess Chess Game Analytics:<br>Patterns of Victory, Openings, and Player Dynamics</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive data-forward analysis of 20,058 Lichess games examining color advantage, rating dynamics, opening effectiveness, and time control patterns.</p>
  <div class="meta">
    <div>Dataset <span>20,058 games</span></div>
    <div>Source <span>Lichess.org via Kaggle</span></div>
    <div>Generated <span>April 2026</span></div>
    <div>Audience <span>Analysts</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- TOC -->
<nav class="toc-bar">
  <ul>
    <li><a href="#overview">1. Dataset Overview</a></li>
    <li><a href="#color-advantage">2. White vs. Black Advantage</a></li>
    <li><a href="#rating-analysis">3. Rating Dynamics</a></li>
    <li><a href="#opening-strategy">4. Opening Analysis</a></li>
    <li><a href="#time-control">5. Time Controls</a></li>
    <li><a href="#key-findings">6. Key Findings</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>

<!-- MAIN -->
<div class="main">
<div class="container">

<!-- ========== SECTION 1: OVERVIEW ========== -->
<section class="section" id="overview">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-num">01</span>
    <h2>Dataset Overview &amp; Game Distribution</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card accent">
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Games</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">20,058</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">All game types included</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Rated Games</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">16,155</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">80.5% of all games</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Unrated / Casual</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">3,903</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">19.5% of all games</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card green">
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg Game Length</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">60.5</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">half-moves (plies)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card orange">
      <div class="kpi-label">Checkmate Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">31.5%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">6,325 games end in mate</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-grid wide">
    <div class="chart-box">
      <h3>Winner Distribution (All 20,058 Games)</h3>
      <canvas id="winnerPieChart" class="short"></canvas>
      <p class="note">Decisive result rate: 95.3% (white wins 49.9%, black wins 45.4%, draws 4.7%)</p>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <h3>Victory Status Breakdown</h3>
      <canvas id="victoryDonut" class="short"></canvas>
      <p class="note">Resignation is by far the most common game-ending, accounting for 55.6% of all outcomes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">
    The dataset spans <strong>20,058 Lichess games</strong>, of which <strong>80.5% are rated</strong> (16,155 games) and the remaining 19.5% are casual/unrated. The overwhelming majority of games end in a decisive result — only <strong>4.7% (950 games)</strong> end in draws, well below the draw rates seen in over-the-board and elite correspondence play.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    By far the most common game termination is <strong>resignation (55.6%)</strong>, followed by <strong>checkmate (31.5%)</strong>, timeout (8.4%), and draws (4.5%). The low draw rate reflects the online blitz/rapid context, where time pressure and playing-strength variance produce decisive results far more frequently than in classical tournament chess.
  </p>

  <!-- Stacked bar: victory status per winner -->
  <div class="chart-box mt">
    <h3>Victory Status by Winner</h3>
    <canvas id="vsWinnerBar" style="max-height:240px;"></canvas>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ========== SECTION 2: COLOR ADVANTAGE ========== -->
<section class="section" id="color-advantage">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-num">02</span>
    <h2>White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Key finding:</strong> White wins 52.3% of all decisive games (excluding draws) across all 20,058 games — a <strong>4.7 percentage-point edge</strong> over black. This advantage is statistically consistent across nearly every rating bracket and time control category analyzed.
  </div>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card green">
      <div class="kpi-label">White Win Rate (decisive)</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">52.3%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">10,001 wins from 19,108 decisive games</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="border-color:var(--black-color)">
      <div class="kpi-label">Black Win Rate (decisive)</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color:var(--black-color)">47.7%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">9,107 wins from 19,108 decisive games</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Advantage Gap</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color:var(--accent2)">+4.7pp</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">White over black (decisive games)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Draw Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">4.7%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">950 draws total</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-grid">
    <div class="chart-box">
      <h3>White Win Rate by Rating Bracket (Decisive Games)</h3>
      <canvas id="ratingBracketBar"></canvas>
      <p class="note">White's edge narrows at higher ratings (50.6% in 2000+ bracket) but persists across all skill levels.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <h3>White Win Rate by Time Control Category</h3>
      <canvas id="tcColorBar"></canvas>
      <p class="note">Bullet games are an outlier (45.7% white wins, n=131). Classical formats show the strongest white edge at 53.4%.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-box">
    <h3>Color Advantage by Victory Status</h3>
    <canvas id="colorByVictoryBar" style="max-height:220px;"></canvas>
    <p class="note">
      White leads in both checkmate (52.9%) and resignation (52.4%) outcomes. The only category where black leads is <strong>outoftime</strong>:
      when games end on the clock, black wins 50.3% of those games — suggesting white may play faster or reach better positions before flagging.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">
    The first-move advantage is a well-documented phenomenon in chess theory, and this dataset confirms it at the amateur online level. White's 52.3% decisive-game win rate is consistent with published statistics from Lichess and Chess.com studies (typically 52–56% across formats). The advantage is present but <strong>not deterministic</strong> — 47.7% of decisive games are won by black.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    Notably, white's advantage <strong>shrinks as rating increases</strong>: in the 2000+ bracket, white wins only 50.6% of decisive games, versus 53.8% in the 1200–1399 range. This suggests that stronger players are better equipped to neutralize white's first-move initiative. The timeout anomaly (black winning 50.3% of timed-out games) is a small but consistent pattern and may reflect time management differences across color assignments.
  </p>

  <!-- Rating bracket detail table -->
  <div class="table-wrap mt">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Avg Rating Bracket</th>
          <th>Games</th>
          <th>White Wins</th>
          <th>Black Wins</th>
          <th>Draws</th>
          <th>White Win% (decisive)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
