<!-- ========== SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS ========== -->
<section class="section" id="key-findings">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-num">06</span>
    <h2>Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</h2>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">
    The following synthesis distills the most actionable patterns from all analyses. Each finding is derived directly from the dataset and has practical implications for players, coaches, and platform designers.
  </p>

  <div class="findings-grid">
    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-num">Finding 01</div>
      <div class="stat">+4.7pp</div>
      <h4>White's First-Move Advantage Is Real But Modest</h4>
      <p>White wins 52.3% of decisive games — a consistent but surmountable edge. The advantage narrows to just 50.6% at 2000+ ratings, confirming that skill increasingly neutralizes color. For players below 1600, color assignment meaningfully affects expected win rate.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-num">Finding 02</div>
      <div class="stat">87.6%</div>
      <h4>Rating Is the Dominant Predictor of Game Outcome</h4>
      <p>At a 500+ Elo gap, the higher-rated player wins 87.6% of decisive games. Even a 200–299 Elo gap produces a 75.2% win rate for the stronger player. For matchmaking purposes, games within 25 Elo are effectively coin flips (51.9%), and rating brackets below 50 Elo are highly competitive.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-num">Finding 03</div>
      <div class="stat">64.2%</div>
      <h4>Van't Kruijs (1.e3) Is the Worst-Performing White Opening</h4>
      <p>The Van't Kruijs Opening gives black a decisive 64.2% win rate — the largest single-opening disadvantage for white in the top 15. The Sicilian Defense (56.6%) and Indian Game (59.2%) also consistently favor black. Opening choice at the amateur level has larger outcome effects than theory would suggest.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-num">Finding 04</div>
      <div class="stat">64.8%</div>
      <h4>Scandinavian Defense: Mieses-Kotroc Is White's Best Weapon</h4>
      <p>White wins 64.8% of decisive games against the Scandinavian Mieses-Kotroc Variation — the highest white win rate among the top 15 openings. The Philidor Defense #3 (65.5%) and Scotch Game (55.8%) also strongly favor white. Players seeking color-advantage maximization should study these lines.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-num">Finding 05</div>
      <div class="stat">55.6%</div>
      <h4>Most Games End in Resignation — Not Checkmate</h4>
      <p>Resignation accounts for 55.6% of all outcomes, with checkmate at 31.5%. At higher ratings (2000+), resignation rises to 67.8% while checkmate drops to 14.4%. Coaches working with lower-rated players should note that 39.8% of sub-1200 games reach checkmate — pattern training in basic mating sequences directly impacts win rate.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-num">Finding 06</div>
      <div class="stat">7.8%</div>
      <h4>Classical Time Controls Produce Markedly More Draws</h4>
      <p>Classical games (&ge;30 min) draw at 7.8%, nearly double the blitz/rapid rate of ~4.2%. Classical games also have the lowest timeout rate (3.5%), confirming that game termination mode is substantially driven by time pressure. For tournament organizers and platform designers, time control is a strong lever for controlling draw frequency.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-num">Finding 07</div>
      <div class="stat">+36%</div>
      <h4>Stronger Players Play Longer, More Complex Games</h4>
      <p>2000+ rated players average 68.1 plies per game versus 50.0 plies for sub-1200s — a 36% difference. This implies deeper opening knowledge, more complex middlegames, and prolonged endgame technique at higher levels. Game length is itself a useful proxy for positional complexity in this dataset.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-num">Finding 08</div>
      <div class="stat">ECO C</div>
      <h4>Open Games (ECO C) Are the Most White-Favorable ECO Family</h4>
      <p>ECO C openings (1.e4 ...e5) show a 54.0% white decisive win rate — the highest of any ECO family. ECO A (flank/irregular) openings are the most balanced at 50.1% white wins, likely reflecting the unpredictable nature of non-standard openings at the amateur level.</p>
    </div>
